 jvp:{n:"Jugular venous pressure",p:"90–92, 146",gm:"cvs",
  how:"Patient at 45°, head on pillows, good lighting, head turned slightly left. Use the right internal jugular vein. Measure the vertical height of the top of the pulsation above the sternal angle. Confirm it is venous: double waveform, falls with inspiration, obliterated then fills from above with light pressure at the base of the neck. Watch for a change with inspiration (Kussmaul's sign).",
  look:">3 cm above the sternal angle = raised right-heart filling pressure (RV failure, volume overload, pericardial disease). Kussmaul's sign: RV infarction, constriction. Large v waves: tricuspid regurgitation."},
